 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Data Requirements:** Monocular video data (one camera view at each time frame) [S1].
-*   **Comparison to ParticleNeRF:** Does not require multi-view data (20 views) [S1]. Uses grid-based field with linear interpolation for efficient querying instead of radius nearest neighbor searching [S1].
-*   **Architecture:** Not tightly coupled to a specific architecture; can be integrated into frameworks like InstantNGP [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+-   **Time Complexity**: Depends on the number of particles and samples. Querying the dynamic field requires iterating over particles within the kernel radius [S1].
+-   **Memory Complexity**: Proportional to the number of particles and the size of the static grid [S1].
+-   **Strengths**: Handles monocular video data; does not require prior geometric knowledge; efficient querying via grid-based interpolation for static parts [S1].
+-   **Limitations**: Performance may degrade in highly dynamic scenes with many particles; binary weight $w_t(x)$ may cause discontinuities [S1].
+-   **When to Use**: Monocular dynamic scene reconstruction where multi-view data is unavailable [S1].
+-   **When Not to Use**: Scenes requiring high-fidelity multi-view consistency or where explicit physical models are available [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was proposed in "Dynamic Appearance Particle Neural Radiance Field" [S1]. It addresses limitations of concurrent works that rely on specific physical models or pre-extracted initial shapes [S1]. It builds on the canonical feature field framework [S1].
